{"alias":"TOP-5500074-A1","dates":{},"display_label":"Math-4008-A1","record":{"artifacts":[{"location":"in_repository","media_type":"text/markdown","path":"files/attempt.md","role":"manuscript"},{"location":"in_repository","media_type":"text/markdown","path":"files/disclosures.md","role":"supplementary"}],"attribution":{"contribution_mode":"ai_only","humans":[{"authorship_confirmation":"confirmed","display_name":"gavenren","github_id":206281466,"login_at_event":"gavenren","person_ref":"per_2d7f91909e614ff78d710c17b6ee185d","roles":["prompting","submission"]}],"organisations":[],"submitter":{"authorship_confirmation":"confirmed","display_name":"gavenren","github_id":206281466,"login_at_event":"gavenren","person_ref":"per_2d7f91909e614ff78d710c17b6ee185d","roles":["prompting","submission"]}},"claim":{"assumptions":[],"limitations":[],"scope_statement":"Determines the recorded least uniform slicing number exactly: f(m)=2m−2 for every integer m≥2. The upper bound follows from Tardos (1995), Theorem 1.2 and Corollary 5.2, through an explicit parameter-preserving reduction to separated two-intervals; at most m−1 vertical and m−1 horizontal lines suffice. An integer-coordinate family with independence number m−1 requires 2m−2 lines, proving sharpness for every m. Includes a finite closed-core argument for arbitrary nonempty interval endpoint conventions, full reduction and sharpness proofs, and two separately written exact coordinate checkers. Resolves the catalogue formulation by established literature; no new extremal theorem or new proof of Tardos’s topological theorem is claimed. No human review or editorial acceptance is asserted.","type":"literature_report"},"contribution_id":"con_c06bc23ab2cd484a98bf66651b4ad12f","declarations":{"ai_disclosure":{"actor":"per_2d7f91909e614ff78d710c17b6ee185d","confirmed":true,"evidence_url":"https://github.com/We-Solve/research/issues/28","recorded_at":"2026-10-18T16:09:40Z","source":"form_field"},"public_disclosure":{"actor":"per_2d